1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:11,852
I am the scribe. I write and revise scientific text in a house style built from one PI's own edits, which your lab can replace with its own. I keep every fact.

2
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:21,477
I revise text you give me, mostly by cutting, and I list every change with its reason. And I draft a section from your results, figures and notes.

3
00:00:21,477 --> 00:00:42,706
I write in your lab's voice by default; nobody has to ask for the house style. I write in whatever form you ask for: the LaTeX of a manuscript, a Word file for a grant, a Markdown page, a letter to an editor, a thesis chapter, a lay summary. And I edit the file itself when you ask.

4
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:59,339
Every fact, number and citation stays as you wrote it; one that looks wrong is flagged for you to check. Where a citation or a number is missing, I mark the place. If the material has a hole, say a number nobody has measured, I answer Gap and say what would fill it.

5
00:00:59,339 --> 00:01:06,776
A lab tailors me by adding its own published papers and its PI's edits as examples.

6
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:12,526
A real run, on a passage written to be bad. The member asks for a revision.

7
00:01:12,526 --> 00:01:24,114
My verdict is Revised. Every number survives, each change is listed with its reason, and the one change to the claim itself is shown for the author to accept or refuse.

8
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:33,820
Say what you want in one plain sentence. Tighten the Discussion and list the changes. Draft the Methods from your notes, with the missing pieces marked.

9
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:45,311
Or write a figure legend for a grant, in Word, under a word limit. Those are examples; ask for any piece of scientific writing, in any form.

10
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:56,831
To use me, just ask Claude Code: ask the scribe to tighten a section, or to draft one from your results and notes. Give me the text and say which part of the paper it is for.

11
00:01:56,831 --> 00:02:07,304
The first line of my reply is the verdict: Revised, Drafted or Gap. Then the text, and what changed and why.
